{
  "passage": "Soil is a very important resource, because almost all our food grows in it. Soil is formed when rocks are slowly broken down by changes in temperature, running water, wind and living things, over thousands of years. India has many types of soil. Alluvial soil is the most widespread. It is deposited by rivers such as the Ganga, the Brahmaputra and the Indus, and it covers the northern plains. It is very fertile and is good for growing rice, wheat and sugarcane. Black soil, also called regur soil, is found in the Deccan plateau, in Maharashtra, Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh. It is formed from volcanic rocks and is excellent for growing cotton. Black soil holds moisture well and develops deep cracks in hot weather. Red and yellow soils develop on old crystalline rocks in the eastern and southern parts of the Deccan. They get their red colour from iron. Laterite soil forms in areas with high temperature and heavy rainfall, where the rain washes away many nutrients. With proper manure, it is used to grow tea, coffee and cashew. Arid soils, found in Rajasthan, are sandy and salty. Soil erosion, the removal of the top layer of soil by wind and water, is a serious problem. Planting trees, terrace farming on slopes and contour ploughing help to prevent it.",
